{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Shall flocks and herds be slaughtered for them.</b> What emerges from Rashi's commentary is as though Moses were in doubt about the power of the El, perish the thought — and because this was said in private, the Holy One, blessed be He, was sparing with him. And all the commentators disagreed with him and sought to justify Moses' words, each according to his own understanding. And the correct explanation among all of them is what emerges from the language of 'וּמָצָא לָהֶם' ('and find for them'), for this is its meaning: that it be slaughtered for them and gathered for them is possible in and of itself; but 'וּמָצָא לָהֶם' — this is not possible. For since they seek nothing but a pretext, even if you give them all the flocks and herds and all the fish of the sea in the world, it will not 'be found for them,' it will not suffice for them, for they will still continue in their rebellion to seek pretexts for matters. And thus the matter appears from the fact that it says: 'אִם אֶת כָּל דְּגֵי הַיָּם יֵאָסֵף לָהֶם וּמָצָא לָהֶם' — two instances of 'לָהֶם,' what do I need them for? To mix them and to teach them: 'אִם אֶת כָּל דְּגֵי הַיָּם יֵאָסֵף וּמָצָא לָהֶם'? Rather, he made two divisions for this reason: because one of them is possible and the second is impossible, since it is a pretext they seek. And it may also be that Rashi too held that Moses was not at all in doubt about the power of the El, blessed be He, and he understood from his words the aforementioned explanation or another explanation of the commentators; but Rashi seized upon him because he spoke his words in language that gives the mistaken room to err and to say that he was in doubt about the power of the El, blessed be He, perish the thought — and from this a profanation of the Name would come. But since it was in private and he was not concerned lest the blind among the camp of the Hebrews hear, therefore he was not particular about his language, seeing that it was with HaShem he was speaking — who understands his intention — and not with the mistaken. But 'Hear now, you skeptics' (Numbers 11:16) was in public, and all Israel heard, as is known; therefore he was punished." "Another matter: he spoke along the way of nature, for by the way of nature they would surely not find in the wilderness enough to supply them with meat and fish except by way of miracle, and according to the magnitude of the miracle they would be liable for great punishment for having imposed trouble through their complaint upon their Creator. And therefore he said 'six hundred thousand footmen' etc., and for so great a people the supply would be a great miracle, and for a month of days — a long time — and you wish to magnify the miracle in order to magnify their punishment; this is impossible. HaShem answered him: If so, they will say that the hand of HaShem is too short; better that they perish than that they say the hand of HaShem is short." "And by way of allusion this verse says — expound me! For one may ask: why do I need 'יִשָּׁחֵט' ('shall be slaughtered') and 'יֵאָסֵף' ('shall be gathered')? Is it now coming to teach us the law of slaughter for animals and gathering for fish? And furthermore, in their complaint they mentioned meat and fish, and the Holy One, blessed be He, gave them a third species — for the bird is not called meat. And in a far-reaching way we may say that he alluded here to what the early Sages said: that the wicked are likened to animals, whose slaughter requires two signs — thus the wicked are banished from this world and the World to Come. And the intermediate are likened to birds, which are rendered fit by one sign — thus they, after receiving their punishment in this world, have a portion in the World to Come. And the righteous are likened to fish, for whom gathering alone suffices; and thus it is said of the righteous: 'וַיֵּאָסֶף אֶל עַמָּיו' (Genesis 25:8), for their death is by a kiss. And here the fire of HaShem had already burned among the extremists and the rejecters, and they did not return to their fat. And the craving ones were the intermediates, as emerges from the language 'אֲשֶׁר בְּקִרְבּוֹ' ('who were among them'), that is, the inside and the middle, and they asked for meat and fish that are not of their kind — for the flesh of an animal befits the wicked and fish the righteous. Therefore he used 'יִשָּׁחֵט' and 'יֵאָסֵף,' and he added the word 'לָהֶם' to say that the animals — in that they require slaughter — and the fish — in that gathering alone suffices — will not 'be found for them': they are not fitting for them, for they are not of their kind at all. And HaShem answered him: 'Now you shall see whether My word will befall you' (Numbers 11:23) — for it is in My power to bring upon them what is of their kind, as emerges from the language of 'יִקְרְךָ' ('will befall you'), for HaShem brought upon them the quail and found species matching species. And all this is by way of derash and far from the plain sense."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:59.650679983Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}